What was changed
----------------

usbhost.c

  - Removed the per-pipe slot table and the lazy interface-claim
    path. Single worker task is now the sole owner of
    usb_host_client_handle_events; it just pumps in a 10 ms-block
    loop and drains the IDF callback queue.
  - submit_xfer is callable from any task. It allocates a
    usb_transfer_t plus a per-call binary done_sem, fills in the
    transfer, calls usb_host_transfer_submit_control or
    usb_host_transfer_submit, and blocks the calling task on
    done_sem with a 50 ms tick. The IDF transfer-completion
    callback fires from inside the worker's event-pump and gives
    done_sem.
  - Multiple URBs can be in flight concurrently because the IDF
    accepts submission from any thread, only event handling has to
    be single-threaded.
  - Cancellation: if *cancel goes high while waiting, the caller
    halts + flushes + clears the endpoint. The IDF reports CANCELED
    via the callback, the caller wakes, returns -ECONNRESET. Halt /
    flush / clear sequence is per the IDF docs (state machine
    requires all three).
  - Interface claim moved to handle_new_device, eagerly. The lazy
    path was deadlocking the cdc-acm open: cdc-acm submits an
    interrupt-IN immediately, that submit triggers the lazy claim,
    which holds state_mutex while it does the IDF API calls, and
    the IDF callbacks for the previous URBs were starved.
  - USBHOST_TRANSFER_TIMEOUT_MS was 5000 for everything. Now 5000
    for control only; bulk and interrupt use 0 (no timeout). The
    cdc-acm interrupt-IN may legitimately pend for seconds waiting
    for a device-side notification; the previous timeout would
    cancel that and confuse the kernel.

usbip_server.c

  - handle_urb_stream becomes a pure read loop. For each
    CMD_SUBMIT it allocates an inflight_urb_t, populates the OUT
    buffer if any, links it into the per-connection inflight list
    (keyed by seqnum), and either runs it inline (synthetic vdev,
    microsecond latency) or pushes it to the per-connection submit
    worker queue.
  - 4 submit worker tasks are spawned per real-device connection.
    Each pulls inflight_urb_t pointers from the queue, calls
    usbhost_*_transfer (now async-friendly), and writes RET_SUBMIT
    under a per-connection tx_mutex. Multiple URBs are concurrent.
  - CMD_UNLINK is real now: looks up the seqnum, sets the cancel
    flag on the matching inflight, and sends RET_UNLINK status=0
    immediately. The submit worker observes cancel inside
    usbhost_*_transfer and the URB completes with -ECONNRESET; the
    worker then sends the corresponding RET_SUBMIT under tx_mutex.
  - Connection teardown: signal cancel for every pending URB, wait
    up to 2 s for inflight_count to drain, then push 4 NULL
    sentinels on the worker queue, wait up to 2 s for the workers
    to acknowledge by signalling workers_done. Mutexes / queues
    are deleted only after workers_done fires (so tx_mutex is
    valid for every worker that may still be executing run_inflight).
  - One-line %zu -> %u fix in handle_devlist_request. IDF
    newlib-nano printf does not implement %zu.

