Why flash failed
----------------

This board's CH340N USB-UART bridge has only RTS wired to EN. DTR is
not wired to GPIO0. Therefore esptool's standard auto-reset (DTR/RTS
toggling) cannot enter the ROM download mode on this hardware:

  RTS=True alone -> POWERON reset (boot:0x8 SPI_FAST_FLASH_BOOT)
  DTR toggling alone -> no observable boot-mode effect

machine.bootloader() from the running firmware DOES enter download
mode (boot:0x0 DOWNLOAD(USB/UART0) confirmed in the ROM banner). A
manually-crafted SLIP sync packet at 115200 baud DOES get a sync
reply (verified: send 0xC0 0x00 0x08 0x24 ... 0xC0 + 32x 0x55 + 0xC0,
get back 7 copies of 0xC0 0x01 0x08 0x04 ... 0xC0). But esptool with
--before no_reset hangs at "Connecting..." regardless of -b 115200
vs -b 460800, --no-stub vs default, --connect-attempts up to 8.
/proc/$pid/io shows it sends 340 bytes then sleeps forever.

I do not understand why esptool fails when manual sync works. The
hypotheses I'd investigate next:

  - Compare the first SLIP frames esptool transmits with what the
    manual probe sends. Capture both via stty -F + cat -v or a
    small pyserial sniffer.
  - Check if the IDF firmware leaves DOWNLOAD(USB/UART0) in a
    half-state where it is listening on USB Serial JTAG (the OTG
    port, currently in host mode with the Pico DUT wired to it) but
    the UART0 path is somehow gated. Try disabling the host setup
    in handle_new_device before calling machine.bootloader() (or
    just don't run usbip_server.start() in test mode, then enter
    bootloader, then flash).
  - The S3 ROM bootloader has a known issue around USB-OTG download
    mode if the OTG was previously configured as host. A full power
    cycle (hardware) recovers it.

Kernel state hazard
-------------------

vhci_hcd port 0000 has a stale attachment (busid 5-1, sockfd 3) from
a prior session. usbip detach -p 0 silently fails. rmmod vhci_hcd
returns "Device or resource busy" because the [vhci_rx] / [vhci_tx]
kernel threads are still alive servicing the dead socket. This is
why pyocd hung at P3.1.4 - it enumerated USB and tried to access the
phantom device. A reboot or careful tracking-down of the userspace
fd holding the socket is needed.

Test environment hazard
-----------------------

mpy-dev list and mpy-dev info both hang on this host (timed out at
30 s and 60 s in this session). mpy-dev tty <label> works (returns
immediately). mpy-dev cycle <label> hangs. So the documented
runbook-power-cycle path via uhubctl is currently inoperative;
physical replug is the fallback. Several uhubctl processes are
running on this host (518xxx range) which might be causing the
contention.

