Architectural notes
-------------------

- The synthetic-fast-path (running run_inflight inline from the read
  loop, skipping the worker pool) preserves the CMSIS-DAP regression
  baseline. Latency is microseconds; CMSIS-DAP never has more than
  one URB in flight at once on the synthetic side.

- Teardown ordering of the connection state matters: workers may be
  inside run_inflight (calling tx_ret_submit which takes tx_mutex)
  when the read loop exits. Teardown waits for inflight_count==0
  (every run_inflight done) BEFORE pushing sentinels and waiting for
  workers_done. tx_mutex is freed only after workers_done fires.

- Inflight back-pressure: read loop blocks (vTaskDelay 2ms) when
  inflight_count >= USBIP_INFLIGHT_MAX (32). The kernel never queues
  that many in practice.

- Stable busid mapping (out-of-scope per the brief, flagging here):
  the real-device busid is 1-N where N = dev_info.dev_addr. That
  counter increments on every re-enumeration, so `usbip attach -r
  host -b 1-1` silently targets a different DUT after the Pico is
  replugged. A stable scheme keyed on iSerialNumber (or a hash of
  it) would solve this. Recommended follow-up.
